  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSurface high pressure placed to our east will allow southwesterly \nreturn flow/moisture increase today. The ever persistent SW ridge \nwill cycle in place through the week before moving our direction \nthis weekend into next week. A shortwave will cross the Great Lakes \ntoday into tomorrow before cycling back into Canada. This will send \na frontal system across our area tomorrow into Friday, providing \nincreased coverage of showers and storms. Following the system, \ntroughing lifts north the last weekend of August, allowing the ridge \nto move in, increase heights, and thus, bring hotter than normal \ntemperatures to welcome in September. Valley highs in the low to mid \n90s and dew points approaching 70 are forecast.\n\nThe increased moisture and slightly warmer temperatures today, will \nallow the right environment for pulse convection to form primarily \nover the higher terrain (Southern Appalachians) today. Shear will be \non the weaker side, and instability will be just enough to support \ndevelopment. Prime timing will be the early afternoon through the \nearly evening hours.\n\nBetter coverage with increased chances elsewhere in the forecast \narea, comes tomorrow as a weak frontal system approaches. \nInstability will be a bit better, as well as shear, but the chance \nfor strong to severe isn't as clear. Thursday morning activity may \nlimit the potential later in the day. Point forecast soundings do \nportray at least a marginal threat. Right now the best chance for \nsevere will be outside of our area to the north and east. However, a \nfew storms may produce strong gusty winds and locally heavy rainfall \nwill also be possible.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage early Friday, surface high pressure \nfills in from the north trying to dry out areas underneath it. The \nNBM, however, does keep some precipitation chances around over the \nweekend and the beginning of next week, but mainly for the\nmountains.",
